From be4ee58be087d122f61566a376d3daed7e9dad8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Desaulniers <ndesaulniers@google.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:17:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [DebugInfo][AT] Avoid accidentally quadratic memory usage in
 AssignmentTrackingAnalysis

Both dataflow passes in AssignmentTrackingAnalysis retained LiveIn and LiveOut
maps for all basic blocks and re-queued forward RPO successors on the initial
pass, scaling quadratically as O(Blocks * Vars) in functions with heavy
inlining.

- Track blocks reachable from backedges (CanRevisit) so acyclic blocks do not
  store LiveIn, free predecessor LiveOut entries once all successors have been
  processed, and avoid re-queuing forward successors already on the RPO
  worklist.
- In MemLocFragmentFill, skip variables without sub-variable fragments, omit
  zero-base intervals from FragsInMemMap, erase empty variable entries, and
  reduce the root IntervalMap leaf capacity from 16 to 4.
- In AssignmentTrackingLowering, derive Assignment::Status from ID, store
  DIAssignID* directly in StackHomeValue, and shrink LocKind to uint8_t
  (52 -> 25 bytes/variable).
- Prefer Small variants of common data structures (SmallBitVector,
  SmallDenseMap, SmallDenseSet, SmallVector) for per-function traversal state.

With this change, I observed a pathological test case (reduced from
glslang/SPIRV) go from ~17.5GB to ~350MB in peak RSS (~50x reduction). I
also verified we did not regress peak RSS in any test under
llvm/test/DebugInfo/Generic/assignment-tracking/ or
llvm/test/DebugInfo/assignment-tracking/.

Link: https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/pull/232504
